  "question": "For $$f(x)=\\sin^{-1}x+\\tan^{-1}x,\\qquad -1<x<1,$$ find $f'(x)$ and evaluate $f'(0)$.",
  "answeroption": "D",
  "explanation": "<b>Differentiation of the given function</b><br>$$f'(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}+\\frac{1}{1+x^2}.$$<br><b>Evaluation at $x=0$</b><br>$$f'(0)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-0^2}}+\\frac{1}{1+0^2}=1+1=2.$$<br>Hence, $$f'(0)=2.$$",
